"Extends a scatterplot with proportional symbol area to encode a third nonnegative quantitative variable.\n\nUse when: Two numeric positions and a third magnitude must be considered together. The size variable spans a manageable range and approximate comparisons suffice.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need precise third-variable comparisons or overlapping circles hide many observations.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal and vertical positions encode two numeric variables. Circle area, rather than radius, encodes the third value; color may identify a small category set.\n\nBuild: Choose the paired position variables and a nonnegative size variable. Map size to circle area using a square-root radius scale. Draw larger circles behind smaller ones and provide a size legend.\n\nCheck: Check area ratios match value ratios. Verify zero-size observations are acknowledged rather than silently disappearing. Confirm units, scale transformations, and the size-legend examples are explicit.\n\nPitfalls: Radius-proportional scaling exaggerates magnitude differences. Intrinsic circle overlap may hide observations; prefer a scatterplot matrix when all three variables need accurate reading.\n\nExample: Plot synthetic projects by duration and cost with circle area representing team size and a labeled size key.\n\nAlternatives: Scatterplot, Scatterplot matrix, Heatmap."
